Title: "Generalized Unions and Intersections"
Id: "986325b0-3dc2-4be3-98cc-d2303192bbe9"


Text: "Binary set operations extend naturally to families containing arbitrarily
       many—even uncountably many—sets. An arbitrary family \mathcal{F} of sets
       has a union \bigcup \mathcal{F} consisting of all elements belonging to
       at least one member set, and an intersection \bigcap \mathcal{F}
       consisting of all elements belonging to every member set."
Id: "aba2ad9f-2f65-4c50-acbd-73af505e944b"


[\arbitrary.union{F}]
Defines: U is \set
when: F is \set
Documented:
. called: "arbitrary union $\bigcup \mathcal{F}$"
. written: "\bigcup \mathcal{F}?"
. description: "The set of all elements belonging to at least one set in family
                \mathcal{F}: the least upper bound in the power set lattice."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{24}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{34}
Id: "eba82141-44f9-4b4e-b44a-102d269592fe"


[\arbitrary.intersection{F}]
Defines: I is \set
when: F is \set
Documented:
. called: "arbitrary intersection $\bigcap \mathcal{F}$"
. written: "\bigcap \mathcal{F}?"
. description: "The set of all elements belonging to every set in non-empty
                family \mathcal{F}: the greatest lower bound in the power set
                lattice."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{25}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{35}
Id: "428c8239-df82-4d86-a74b-75129fbdaea3"


Text: "When the indexing family is empty, existential and universal
       quantification yield dual boundary behaviors: the union over an empty
       family is empty, while the intersection over an empty subfamily of a
       universe U is the entire universe U."
Id: "6981d915-aeb8-4614-b9cc-d16ee2373f02"


Theorem:
given: U is \set
then: (\arbitrary.union{\empty.set} = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "union of the empty family"
. written: "\bigcup \emptyset = \emptyset"
. description: "Because the empty family contains no member sets, its union is
                unconditionally empty."
Proof: """An element $x$ belongs to $\bigcup \emptyset$ if and only if there
          exists a set $S \in \emptyset$ such that $x \in S$. Because
          $\emptyset$ contains no sets, this existential condition is
          unconditionally false for every object $x$. Hence
          $\bigcup \emptyset = \emptyset$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{24}
Id: "4c132c09-9639-481b-b8f0-f11c9cc92147"


Text: "De Morgan's Laws generalize directly to arbitrary families: the
       complement of an arbitrary union is the arbitrary intersection of the
       individual complements, and the complement of an arbitrary intersection
       is the arbitrary union of the individual complements."
Id: "a2d68549-352a-4690-86e4-bf1f0ab74181"


[\generalized.de.morgan.union]
Theorem:
given: F, U is \set
then:
. (\absolute.complement:of{\arbitrary.union{F}}:in{U} is \set)
Documented:
. called: "Generalized De Morgan's Law for Unions"
. written: "\left(\bigcup_{i \in I} A_i\right)^c = \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i^c"
. description: "The complement of an arbitrary union equals the intersection of
                the individual complements."
Proof: """An element $x \in U$ belongs to $(\bigcup \mathcal{F})^c \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in \bigcup \mathcal{F})
          \iff x \in U \land \neg(\exists S \in \mathcal{F}, x \in S)$.
          By quantifier duality, $\neg \exists S \equiv \forall S \neg$,
          so this is equivalent to $x \in U \land \forall S \in \mathcal{F}, x \notin S
          \iff \forall S \in \mathcal{F}, (x \in U \land x \notin S)
          \iff \forall S \in \mathcal{F}, x \in U \setminus S$.
          Therefore, $x \in \bigcap \{S^c \mid S \in \mathcal{F}\}$,
          establishing $(\bigcup \mathcal{F})^c = \bigcap \{S^c \mid S \in \mathcal{F}\}$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{26}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{36}
Id: "b2570a6f-b43f-4ca7-9e29-6f0b1ef2ec6c"


Text: "Set intersection distributes across arbitrary unions, allowing a fixed
       set to be factored into every component of an arbitrary family."
Id: "fd14f3c7-73d1-4eff-861a-ac655216caba"


Theorem:
given: B, F is \set
then: ((B \.cap./ \arbitrary.union{F}) is \set)
Documented:
. called: "generalized distributivity of intersection over union"
. written: "B \cap \bigcup \mathcal{F} = \bigcup \{B \cap S \mid S \in
            \mathcal{F}\}"
. description: "Intersecting a set with an arbitrary union distributes across
                every component set."
Proof: """An element $x$ belongs to $B \cap \bigcup \mathcal{F}$ if and only if
          $x \in B$ and $x \in \bigcup \mathcal{F}$, which means $x \in B$ and
          there exists $S \in \mathcal{F}$ such that $x \in S$. This is
          equivalent to the existence of $S \in \mathcal{F}$ such that
          $x \in B \land x \in S$, which is $x \in B \cap S$. Thus
          $x \in \bigcup \{B \cap S \mid S \in \mathcal{F}\}$, proving
          distributivity."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{26}
Id: "1870622d-c6f4-4cd3-b128-37c3f88dcb85"


Text: "A family of sets \mathcal{F} is pairwise disjoint (or mutually disjoint)
       if no two distinct member sets share any elements in common: for any A, B
       \in \mathcal{F} with A \neq B, their intersection is empty."
Id: "f0b5d7f7-8b84-4272-bf9b-10c237a1d66b"


[\pairwise.disjoint{F}]
States:
when: F is \set
that:
. forAll: A, B is \set
  where:
  . A "in"? F
  . B "in"? F
  . A != B
  then: ((A \.cap./ B) = \empty.set)
Documented:
. called: "pairwise disjoint family"
. written: "\operatorname{Disjoint}(\mathcal{F}?)"
. description: "The condition asserting that every pair of distinct sets in
                family \mathcal{F} has empty intersection."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{27}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{15}
Id: "1ac27195-7d8d-49a6-8358-65de1844d005"


Theorem:
given: F, G is \set
where:
. \pairwise.disjoint{F}
. G \.subseteq./ F
then:
. \pairwise.disjoint{G}
Documented:
. called: "hereditary property of pairwise disjointness"
. written: "G \subseteq \mathcal{F} \land \operatorname{Disjoint}(\mathcal{F})
            \implies \operatorname{Disjoint}(G)"
. description: "Any subfamily of a pairwise disjoint family is itself pairwise
                disjoint."
Proof: """Let $G \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ with $\mathcal{F}$ pairwise disjoint.
          Let $A, B \in G$ be distinct sets ($A \ne B$). Because
          $G \subseteq \mathcal{F}$, both $A \in \mathcal{F}$ and
          $B \in \mathcal{F}$. Since $\mathcal{F}$ is pairwise disjoint,
          $A \ne B$ implies $A \cap B = \emptyset$. Because $A$ and $B$ were
          arbitrary distinct members of $G$, family $G$ is pairwise disjoint."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{27}
Id: "d11bceba-fbb4-44d6-ad76-7c4621e24094"


Text: "A partition of a set X is a collection \mathcal{P} of non-empty, pairwise
       disjoint subsets of X whose union equals X. Partitions represent the
       canonical set-theoretic decomposition of a universe into non-overlapping
       cells, providing the geometric foundation for equivalence relations and
       quotient structures."
Id: "9fcdae4c-2d57-4b46-8443-860346940eab"


[\set.partition:of{X}]
Declares: P is \set
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: A is \set
  where: (A "in"? P)
  then: (A != \empty.set)
. \pairwise.disjoint{P}
. \arbitrary.union{P} = X
Documented:
. called: "partition of set $X?$"
. written: "\operatorname{Partition}(X?)"
. description: "A family of non-empty, pairwise disjoint subsets of set X whose
                union equals X."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{27}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{28}
Id: "bcb44efa-4b22-4344-91d4-5f8a11cda366"


Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. P is \set.partition:of{X}
then:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: (x "in"? X)
Documented:
. called: "unique block membership of partitions"
. written: "\forall x \in X, \exists! A \in \mathcal{P}, x \in A"
. description: "Every element of set X belongs to exactly one block of partition
                \mathcal{P}."
Proof: """Let $x \in X$. Since $\mathcal{P}$ is a partition of $X$,
          $\bigcup \mathcal{P} = X$. Because $x \in X = \bigcup \mathcal{P}$,
          there exists at least one block $A \in \mathcal{P}$ such that
          $x \in A$. To establish uniqueness, suppose there exists another block
          $B \in \mathcal{P}$ with $x \in B$. Then $x \in A \cap B$, so
          $A \cap B \neq \emptyset$. By pairwise disjointness of $\mathcal{P}$,
          distinct blocks must be disjoint; hence $A \cap B \neq \emptyset$
          forces $A = B$. Thus each element of $X$ belongs to a unique block of
          $\mathcal{P}$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{28}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{29}
Id: "ecd4dc40-4684-41da-af7a-0a689204852a"


Text: "Partitions and equivalence relations represent two perspectives on the
       same mathematical phenomenon: every partition \mathcal{P} of X defines an
       equivalence relation where two elements are equivalent if and only if
       they occupy the same block, and every equivalence relation partitions its
       domain into mutually disjoint equivalence classes."
Id: "ef0adebb-b584-46e0-a8f6-0d088573cf35"
